.\" pi-gen-micro-sysroot - build pi-gen-micro images without Raspberry Pi OS or root
.TH PI\-GEN\-MICRO\-SYSROOT 1 "2026-08-25" "pi-gen-micro" "Raspberry Pi Tools"
.SH NAME
pi\-gen\-micro\-sysroot \- build pi\-gen\-micro images on any Linux host, without root
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B pi\-gen\-micro\-sysroot create
.br
.B pi\-gen\-micro\-sysroot run
.I configuration
.RI [ target_devices ]
.br
.B pi\-gen\-micro\-sysroot shell
.RI [ command ...]
.br
.B pi\-gen\-micro\-sysroot update
.br
.B pi\-gen\-micro\-sysroot clean
.br
.B pi\-gen\-micro\-sysroot remove
.br
.B pi\-gen\-micro\-sysroot status
.SH DESCRIPTION
.BR pi\-gen\-micro (1)
assumes it is running on Raspberry Pi OS as root: it needs a Debian userland of
the image's own architecture, the Debian and Raspberry Pi archive keyrings, and
.BR rpi\-make\-boot\-image (1)
and
.B rpi\-modcopy
\(em which only the Raspberry Pi archive publishes.
.PP
.B pi\-gen\-micro\-sysroot
removes both assumptions. It bootstraps a throwaway Debian sysroot with
.BR mmdebstrap (1),
installs the Raspberry Pi archive tooling into it, and runs
.BR pi\-gen\-micro (1)
inside it in an unprivileged user namespace. Nothing is installed on the host.
.PP
A sysroot is only bootstrapped when the host needs one. Where the host is
already the image's architecture and has pi\-gen\-micro's tooling \(em a
Raspberry Pi, or any Debian of that architecture \(em the build runs in place
and only the namespace is set up, so nothing is downloaded.
.B USE_SYSROOT
overrides the choice and
.B status
reports which mode applies.
.PP
The namespace is worth having even in place:
.I delete.list
and
.I generic_delete.list
are applied with an unquoted, glob-expanded
.BR rm (1),
which under
.BR sudo (8)
runs as real root against the host.
.PP
The sysroot is created once and reused. It is roughly 150\ MB, lives outside the
source tree, and can be deleted at any time with
.BR "pi\-gen\-micro\-sysroot remove" .
.SS Architecture
The sysroot's architecture must match the image's, and defaults to it
.RI ( arm64 ).
This is not a matter of taste:
.BR dpkg (1)
has its native architecture compiled in and cannot be told to treat another as
native, so an amd64
.B dpkg
sees the image's arm64 packages as foreign and can never satisfy the
unqualified dependencies of an
.B "Architecture: all"
package from them. On an arm64 host the sysroot is native; elsewhere it runs
under
.BR qemu\-user (1)
via
.BR binfmt_misc (5),
which must already be registered on the host \(em install
.B qemu\-user\-static
and
.B binfmt\-support
there.
.SS Privileges
The build runs as uid 0 inside a user namespace that maps the invoking user to
root and their
.BR subuid (5)
and
.BR subgid (5)
ranges to 1..N. Mapping the range matters, because
.BR dpkg (1)
chowns unpacked files to non-root ids; mapping the caller (rather than a subuid)
to root matters too, so that build output is owned by the caller afterwards.
An entry in
.I /etc/subuid
and
.I /etc/subgid
is therefore required, along with
.BR newuidmap (1)
from
.BR uidmap .
Without them the build falls back to a namespace that maps only uid 0, and
package installs that chown to other ids will fail.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B create
Bootstrap the build sysroot. A no-op if one is already present, or if the host
is suitable and the build will run in place. Called implicitly by
.B run
and
.BR shell .
.TP
.BI run " configuration " [ target_devices ]
Run
.BR pi\-gen\-micro (1)
inside the sysroot with the given arguments. The current directory is
bind-mounted at the same path inside, so output is written to
.I $PWD/out_image/
exactly as it would be for a native build.
.TP
.BR shell " [\fIcommand\fR ...]"
Run a command \(em by default an interactive shell \(em inside the sysroot.
Useful for inspecting a failed build.
.TP
.B update
.BR apt\-get (8)
update and dist-upgrade the sysroot.
.TP
.B clean
Remove
.BR pi\-gen\-micro (1)
build artefacts from the current directory. Prefer this to
.BR rm (1):
.BR dpkg (1)
chowns unpacked files to non-root ids, which land as subuids on the host and
cannot be unlinked from outside the namespace. Rebuilding does not require it \(em
.BR pi\-gen\-micro (1)
clears its own
.I build/
and
.I dpkg_admin/
on each run.
.TP
.B remove
Delete the sysroot.
.TP
.B status
Print the sysroot's paths, suite, architecture and state.
.SH NATIVE HELPERS
Emulation costs are concentrated in the arch-neutral stages:
.B zstd \-\-ultra \-22
on a 70\ MB initramfs takes about 21 seconds natively against some nine minutes
emulated.
.PP
.B zstd
and
.B cpio
only transform bytes, so the sysroot uses the host's builds of them.
.B create
copies each host binary into
.I /native
together with its libraries, and writes a wrapper that invokes it through the
host's dynamic loader \(em so the sysroot's glibc and the host's need not agree.
.I /native/bin
is first on
.BR PATH .
A tool absent from the host is left emulated, and
.B status
reports which helpers are in place.
.PP
Everything that touches packages \(em
.BR dpkg (1),
.BR apt (8),
maintainer scripts,
.BR ldconfig (8),
.BR depmod (8)
\(em deliberately stays native to the image. Only tools that transform data
belong in
.BR NATIVE_TOOLS .
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B USE_SYSROOT
Whether to build in a sysroot:
.I auto
(default) bootstraps one only when the host is unsuitable,
.I 1
always bootstraps one,
.I 0
never does and builds in place.
.TP
.B SYSROOT_DIR
Sysroot location. Default:
.IR ${XDG_CACHE_HOME:\-$HOME/.cache}/pi\-gen\-micro/sysroot\-<suite>\-<arch> .
.TP
.B SYSROOT_DIST
Debian suite for the sysroot. Default:
.IR trixie .
Keep this in sync with
.IR /var/lib/pi\-gen\-micro/internal/apt/sources.list.d/ ,
so that the sysroot's apt and the image's apt agree on what the suite means.
.TP
.B SYSROOT_ARCH
Sysroot architecture. Default:
.BR TARGET_ARCH .
.TP
.B TARGET_ARCH
Architecture of the image being built. Default:
.IR arm64 .
.TP
.B DEBIAN_MIRROR
Default:
.IR http://deb.debian.org/debian .
.TP
.B DEBIAN_SECURITY_MIRROR
Default:
.IR http://deb.debian.org/debian\-security .
.TP
.B RPI_MIRROR
Default:
.IR http://archive.raspberrypi.com/debian .
.TP
.B KEYRING_CACHE
Where fetched archive keyrings are cached. Default:
.IR ${XDG_CACHE_HOME:\-$HOME/.cache}/pi\-gen\-micro/keyrings .
.TP
.B PI_GEN_MICRO_ROOT
Where to find configurations, helper packages and internal data when not
running from a source checkout. Default:
.IR /var/lib/pi\-gen\-micro .
.PP
When run from a git checkout,
.B pi\-gen\-micro\-sysroot
detects this and points
.BR pi\-gen\-micro (1)
at the checkout, so edits to configurations and helper packages take effect
without reinstalling the package.
.SH KEYRINGS
Archive keyrings are needed before apt can verify anything, so they are fetched
over the network and then checked against fingerprints recorded in the script:
.PP
.RS
.nf
04B54C3CDCA79751B16BC6B5225629DF75B188BD  Debian 13 (trixie) archive
CF8A1AF502A2AA2D763BAE7E82B129927FA3303E  Raspberry Pi archive
.fi
.RE
.PP
Published at
.I https://ftp\-master.debian.org/keys.html
and
.I https://archive.raspberrypi.com/debian/raspberrypi.gpg.key
respectively. A host keyring is reused when it already contains the expected
key. Adding a suite means adding its fingerprint to
the
.B case
on
.B SYSROOT_DIST
in the script.
.PP
The Raspberry Pi archive key carries SHA1 self-certifications, which apt on
trixie \(em which verifies with Sequoia rather than
.BR gpgv (1)
\(em rejects from 2026-02-01 onwards. The sysroot therefore ships a relaxed
hash policy in
.IR /etc/crypto\-policies/back\-ends/apt\-sequoia.config ;
without it every
.I archive.raspberrypi.com
fetch inside the sysroot fails as unsigned.
.SH EXAMPLES
Build the fastboot image for CM5 and Pi 5 on a non-Raspberry Pi OS host:
.PP
.RS
.nf
mkdir \-p ~/build && cd ~/build
pi\-gen\-micro\-sysroot run fastboot cm5,pi5
.fi
.RE
.PP
Inspect the sysroot, then throw it away:
.PP
.RS
.nf
pi\-gen\-micro\-sysroot status
pi\-gen\-micro\-sysroot shell
pi\-gen\-micro\-sysroot remove
.fi
.RE
.PP
Build against a local mirror:
.PP
.RS
.nf
DEBIAN_MIRROR=http://mirror.example/debian \\
RPI_MIRROR=http://mirror.example/raspberrypi \\
    pi\-gen\-micro\-sysroot create
.fi
.RE
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /var/lib/pi\-gen\-micro/internal/sysroot/packages
Host build dependencies installed into the sysroot.
.TP
.I /etc/pi\-gen\-micro\-sysroot
Written inside a sysroot to record the suite and architecture it was built for.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR pi\-gen\-micro (1),
.BR mmdebstrap (1),
.BR unshare (1),
.BR newuidmap (1),
.BR subuid (5),
.BR user_namespaces (7)
